<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyrical Ballads</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lyrical Ballads, with a Few Other Poems</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lyrical Ballads is a collection of poems by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> and Samuel Taylor Coleridge, first published in 1798 and generally considered to have marked the beginning of the English Romantic movement. Coleridge's four poems in the first edition, about a third of its length, included The Rime of the Ancient Mariner; the other nineteen were Wordsworth's. The poets set out to overturn what they considered the learned, highly sculpted forms of 18th-century English poetry and to write in common, everyday language. Wordsworth's preface to the 1800 edition became known as the manifesto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads.jpg" alt="Lyrical Ballads" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Wordsworth (1770-1850) and Samuel Taylor Coleridge (1772-1834). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyrical_Ballads.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
